      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Sam Houston Park on Bagby Street houses the Houston Heritage Society, preserving historic houses that tell the city’s early story.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Downtown_Houston",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the Galveston Hurricane of 1900, investors shifted to Downtown Houston, seeking a safer port location away from hurricane‑hit Galveston.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Downtown_Houston",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "From 1971 to 2018, about forty Downtown buildings earned spots on the National Register of Historic Places, reflecting the area’s rich heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Downtown_Houston",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Enron’s 40‑floor skyscraper, completed in 2002, stood as Houston’s tallest building until the company collapsed two years later.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Downtown_Houston",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Historically, Houston Industries and its subsidiary Houston Power & Lighting kept their headquarters in Downtown, shaping the city’s energy legacy.",
